{"review_id": "REV-12", "question_id": "63850ac98a47ae49f0f49c1c1a6e45c6c447272c", "paper_arxiv_id": "1908.10383", "manifest_item_index": 276, "actual_model": "qwen2.5:0.5b", "blind_label_in_signed_sheet": "Model_Alpha", "model_by_original_mapping": "qwen2.5:0.5b", "attribution_changed": false, "match_method": "exact", "final_primary_label": "UNSUPPORTED", "final_secondary_label": "", "is_adjudicated_change": "NO", "original_primary_label": "UNSUPPORTED", "is_refusal": false, "token_f1": 0.0661, "automated_containment_proxy": 0.0, "latency_s": 4.4638, "tokens_per_second": 55.64, "answer": "The problem with existing metrics that they are trying to address is that they are not designed to accurately reflect the quality of the extracted text. These metrics are typically based on the precision, recall, and F1 score, which are not specific to the quality of the extracted text. For example, the precision measures the number of true positive results, while the recall measures the number of true positive results divided by the total number of true positives. These metrics do not account for the quality of the extracted text, which can be subjective and can vary from one user to another."}
